{
  "script": [
    {
      "beatId": "b1",
      "narration": "In March twenty twenty-five, Larkspur Pay's checkout started failing. Thirty-one percent of requests failed, for forty-seven minutes, after the limiter's store slowed.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Why a slow store broke checkout",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Larkspur Pay",
        "Checkout failing",
        "Slow store"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "31%",
          "label": "Checkouts failed",
          "sourceRef": "num-19"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Larkspur Pay platform team, 14 March 2025"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b2",
      "narration": "A rate limiter refuses extra requests. Here it's a token bucket, a bucket of permission tokens. Each checkout spends one. The bucket holds a hundred tokens, refilling ten per second.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Token bucket = permission tokens",
        "semantic": "concept"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Spend one token",
        "Refill rate"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "100 tokens",
          "label": "Bucket capacity",
          "sourceRef": "num-1"
        }
      ],
      "source": "A field guide to token-bucket rate limiting"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b3",
      "narration": "But the gateway runs twenty-four nodes. A bucket per node would allow twenty-four times the limit. So every node reads the same count from one shared store.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "One shared bucket per customer",
        "semantic": "decision"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Per-node buckets",
        "One shared count"
      ]
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b4",
      "narration": "Here two nodes race for the last token, both would pass a request. The store's atomic script, one indivisible step, lets one spend it. The other gets four-two-nine.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Atomic script = no double spend",
        "semantic": "concept"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Last token",
        "Only one wins"
      ]
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b5",
      "narration": "But that shared store adds a network round trip. Median cost is zero point four milliseconds. Past the fifty millisecond timeout, a call counts as a failure.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Timeout flips the failure policy",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Round trip",
        "Store failure"
      ],
      "source": "quotaflow"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b6",
      "narration": "After the failover, calls took eighty milliseconds, past the timeout. The limiter failed open, so forty instances each ran the full limit. Traffic hit forty times the contracted rate.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Fail open multiplies the limit",
        "semantic": "problem"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Fail open",
        "Local limits"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "40×",
          "label": "Over the contracted rate",
          "sourceRef": "num-23"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Larkspur Pay platform team, 14 March 2025"
    },
    {
      "beatId": "b7",
      "narration": "So Larkspur made the checkout limiter fail closed, refusing what it cannot count. Local fallback limits are divided by the instance count. The store timeout rose to a hundred fifty milliseconds.",
      "headline": {
        "text": "Fail closed, split the fallback",
        "semantic": "resolution"
      },
      "tags": [
        "Fail closed",
        "Split fallback"
      ],
      "statCards": [
        {
          "value": "150 ms",
          "label": "New store timeout",
          "sourceRef": "num-25"
        }
      ],
      "source": "Post-incident report: checkout errors on 14 March 2025"
    }
  ]
}

